From 64c5dd1cbdea299a8ee7712f319780bf9f94acb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu Duponchelle <mathieu.duponchelle@opencreed.com>
Date: Tue, 16 Jul 2019 01:02:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sctp_output: fix non-FreeBSD compilation (#324)

Fixes an issue introduced in f4166b347188af0de05fa70456042ef8a792cdd7,
the scope must be closed outside of the FreeBSD clause, as it is opened
outside of it.

diff --git a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
index 713a304..d0e8f27 100755
--- a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
+++ b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_output.c
@@ -13868,8 +13868,8 @@ sctp_lower_sosend(struct socket *so,
 		if (flags & MSG_EOF) {
 			sinfo_flags |= SCTP_EOF;
 		}
-	}
 #endif
+	}
 	if (sinfo_flags & SCTP_ADDR_OVER) {
 		if (addr)
 			net = sctp_findnet(stcb, addr);
-- 
2.25.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
#if defined(__FreeBSD__)
		if (flags & MSG_EOF) {
			sinfo_flags |= SCTP_EOF;
		}
	}
#endif
```
Reason:
The closing brace for the conditional block checking `flags & MSG_EOF` is placed inside the FreeBSD-specific preprocessor directive, which restricts the scope of this logic to only FreeBSD systems. As a result, non-FreeBSD systems do not compile this logic, leading to compilation issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
	}
#endif
	if (sinfo_flags & SCTP_ADDR_OVER) {
```
Reason:
By moving the closing brace outside of the FreeBSD clause, the logic for `flags & MSG_EOF` is now correctly applied to all systems, ensuring that the `sinfo_flags` variable is set appropriately regardless of the operating system, thus resolving the compilation issues on non-FreeBSD systems.

